  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1022 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAbove-normal moisture (PWs 1.2-1.4\") remains for today with mid-\nlevel clouds clearing out by the afternoon, similar to yesterday. \nThe ridge axis from the central Plains is still in place, resulting \nin a bit of subsidence and limiting storm initiation. Storm chances \nwill be more favored E of the RGV this afternoon where there's \nbetter moisture/instability (SBCAPE 500-1000 J/kg). Slow storm \nmotions and high rain rates may result in isolated flash flooding, \nmost likely in the NW Sacs where the HREF has max rainfall up to 2\".\nOutflow gusts over 30 mph are possible today as the convection \ndissipates in the evening. \n\nThe upper high moves a bit south on Sun over OK as forcing remains \nlimited. 90th %-ile PWs and the best storm chances shift N and W \ntomorrow. Mostly dry conditions are expected along and E of the RGV \nfor Sun with isolated storm coverage, excluding the Sacs where \nscattered storms are forecast. Similar conditions are forecast for \nMon with little change in the upper high's placement and the \nmonsoonal moisture plume sitting over western areas.\n\nThe upper high stretches across the southern tier of the US through \nmidweek. The easterly flow underneath the high will nudge the \nmoisture plume further S and W, but PWs hold in the 1.1-1.2\" range, \nenough to give most of the lowlands at least a slight chance of \nstorms through Thu. The heavy rain threat should lower out west \nafter Mon. As an inverted trough approaches from the east around \nmidweek, the high is modeled to centralize over the Desert SW. This \nfeature would keep the trough south of our area late in the period, \nbut recycled moisture around the ridge keeps low-end storm chances \nalive into next weekend. \n\nTemperatures will be near or slightly above normal through the \nperiod with lowland highs in the low-mid 90s. Relatively warm temps \nare forecast for eastern areas this week.",
